IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `rtw-dates`
IMPORT `rtw-core`
IMPORT `rtw-seat`

-- Return to Work Act 1986 (NT) -- cases for the worker's seat (rtw-seat.l4).
--
-- An illustrative worker, Ana: a storeperson at a Darwin warehouse, employed on PAYG by a company insured with an approved
-- insurer, born 15 June 1985. She hurts her back lifting on Monday 2 March 2026 and gives her employer her claim, with a
-- doctor's certificate, on Monday 9 March 2026. Her pension age under the Social Security Act 1991 (Cth) s 23(5A) is 67
-- (804 months: born on or after 1 January 1957). And an illustrative death: Ben, a mechanic, dies of a work injury,
-- leaving a dependent spouse and two children aged 8 and 12. Average weekly earnings are ILLUSTRATIVE: $2,000.
-- Every expected value was worked by hand from the deposited text, with the arithmetic in the comment above it.

`AWE` MEANS 2000

§ `Am I covered?`

-- Ana: under a contract, a PAYG employee, nothing that takes her out.
#ASSERT `Am I a worker under the Return to Work Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 72800 `in neither prescribed class` `AWE`
-- A courier on an ABN, not an employee for PAYG in substance: not a worker.
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I a worker under the Return to Work Act` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 60000 `in neither prescribed class` `AWE`)
-- A juror: prescribed as a worker by reg 3A(1)(aa).
#ASSERT `Am I a worker under the Return to Work Act` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `prescribed as a worker by reg 3A(1)` `AWE`

-- Ana's back injury lifting at work: compensable.
#ASSERT `Is my injury compensable under the Return to Work Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 72800 `in neither prescribed class` `the Territory` `the Territory` `the Territory` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `compensable under Part 5`
-- Had she been hurt in a car crash driving home from her usual workplace: s 4(2A)(b) takes it out, and the Motor
-- Accidents (Compensation) Act covers it instead.
#ASSERT `Is my injury compensable under the Return to Work Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 72800 `in neither prescribed class` `the Territory` `the Territory` `the Territory` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `not compensable: a motor accident in a recess or on a journey that s 4(2A) takes out`
-- Had she been driving a forklift on the public road at 0.10: only death benefits, medical costs and rehabilitation.
#ASSERT `Is my injury compensable under the Return to Work Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 72800 `in neither prescribed class` `the Territory` `the Territory` `the Territory` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE 0.10 FALSE FALSE FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `compensable only for death benefits, medical and other costs and rehabilitation (s 60(3))`
-- A fly-in fly-out worker usually working in Western Australia: not connected with the Territory.
#ASSERT `Is my injury compensable under the Return to Work Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 72800 `in neither prescribed class` `another State or Territory` `the Territory` `the Territory` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `not compensable: the employment is not connected with the Territory (ss 53(2), 53AA)`

-- For the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act s 10A(b): Ana's injury is compensable here outright, so the "but for s 57"
-- question is no. A worker hurt by her own serious and wilful misconduct, with no long-term incapacity, is yes.
#ASSERT NOT (`Would my injury be compensable but for s 57` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Would my injury be compensable but for s 57` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Would my injury be compensable but for s 57` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Would my injury be compensable but for s 57` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE)

-- Claim 9 March 2026, a week after the injury: maintainable. The 6 months run to 2 September 2026.
#ASSERT `Can proceedings on my claim be maintained` (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `maintainable: notice given and the claim made within 6 months (s 182(1))`
#ASSERT `By when must I make my claim` (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 2 9 2026
-- Claimed 10 September 2026 with no reasonable cause: not maintainable.
#ASSERT `Can proceedings on my claim be maintained` (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 10 9 2026) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not maintainable: the claim was not made within 6 months and no reasonable cause is found (s 182(1), (3))`

§ `What will I be paid each week, and until when?`

-- $1,400 ordinary pay, $150 regular overtime, $50 leading hand allowance: $1,600.
#ASSERT `What are my normal weekly earnings` 1400 0 150 0 50 0 FALSE `AWE` EQUALS 1600
-- Week 1, nothing paid yet, certified unfit, earning nothing: $1,600 under s 64.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 0 TRUE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `s 64 compensation, in dollars for the week` 1600
-- Week 10 on light duties earning $600: $1,600 - $600 = $1,000.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 9 TRUE FALSE 1600 600 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `s 64 compensation, in dollars for the week` 1000
-- Week 30 (29 paid): indexed $1,680, capable of $600, loss $1,080; (1B) 75% = $810. Her spouse is dependent and she has
-- two prescribed children, so the (12) amount is $2,000 x 75% = $1,500; 75% of $1,680 = $1,260 is less, so (8) applies:
-- the lesser of 90% of $1,080 = $972 and $1,500 / $1,680 x $1,080 = $964.29 (exactly 6750/7).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 29 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `s 65 compensation, in dollars for the week` (6750 DIVIDED BY 7)
-- The same week if her spouse earned $3,200 a week (over 150% of $2,000): no floor; $810.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 29 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 3200 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `s 65 compensation, in dollars for the week` 810
-- Week 261, with a 10% impairment: nothing more.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 260 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `none: the 260 weeks have been paid (ss 65(1BA), 61A(2)(a))`
-- Moved to New Zealand without the reg 6A declarations: suspended.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 40 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `none: suspended while living outside Australia (s 65B)`
-- Paid 104 weeks in Darwin, then moves to New Zealand, with the declarations and her employer satisfied: only her first
-- 26 weeks count under s 65B(3) (finding 19), so she is still paid, as in week 30: 6750/7 dollars. Had 78 of her weeks
-- after the 26th been paid while she lived in New Zealand, 26 + 78 = 104 would count, and the entitlement would have ceased.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 104 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 `AWE` EQUALS `s 65 compensation, in dollars for the week` (6750 DIVIDED BY 7)
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation do I get this week` 104 FALSE FALSE 1600 0 1680 600 TRUE 2 0 FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 78 `AWE` EQUALS `none: suspended while living outside Australia (s 65B)`
-- READING: $1,600 x $2,100 / $2,000 = $1,680.
#ASSERT `What are my indexed normal weekly earnings on the N x A / C reading` 1600 2100 2000 EQUALS 1680

-- Born 15 June 1985: pension age 67 on 15 June 2052; retiring age in warehousing 65 (780 months), not later; injured
-- 2 March 2026, more than 2 years before; impairment 10%: 260 weeks, or 15 June 2052 if earlier (s 65(1D)(b)(ii)).
#ASSERT `Until when will my weekly compensation be paid` (`the calendar date` 15 6 1985) (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) 804 780 FALSE EQUALS `until 260 weeks have been paid, or future pension age on this day if that is earlier` (`the calendar date` 15 6 2052)
-- With 15% or more: until 15 June 2052 (s 65(1D)(a)(ii)).
#ASSERT `Until when will my weekly compensation be paid` (`the calendar date` 15 6 1985) (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) 804 780 TRUE EQUALS `until the worker attains future pension age, on` (`the calendar date` 15 6 2052)

-- s 61B(4): notice posted 1 August 2026 of the end of her 26 weeks on 7 September: the change takes effect 7 September.
-- A notice posted only on 1 September: 15 September.
#ASSERT `When does a change to my weekly compensation take effect` (`the calendar date` 1 8 2026) (`the calendar date` 7 9 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 7 9 2026
#ASSERT `When does a change to my weekly compensation take effect` (`the calendar date` 1 9 2026) (`the calendar date` 7 9 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 15 9 2026
-- s 61A(2)(a)(ii): her 260th week ends 28 February 2031; costs to 28 February 2032.
#ASSERT `Until when can my costs still be paid after the 260 weeks` (`the calendar date` 28 2 2031) EQUALS `the calendar date` 28 2 2032
-- s 89: due 22 March 2026, paid 1 April (10 days late): 2 weeks.
#ASSERT `How many weeks late was my weekly payment` (`the calendar date` 22 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 1 4 2026) EQUALS 2

§ `What lump sum do I get for a permanent impairment?`

-- 10%: 3% of 208 x $2,000 = $12,480. 14%: 12% = $49,920. 15%: $62,400. 3%: none.
#ASSERT `How much do I get for my permanent impairment` 10 `AWE` EQUALS `a lump sum, in dollars, of` 12480
#ASSERT `How much do I get for my permanent impairment` 14 `AWE` EQUALS `a lump sum, in dollars, of` 49920
#ASSERT `How much do I get for my permanent impairment` 15 `AWE` EQUALS `a lump sum, in dollars, of` 62400
#ASSERT `How much do I get for my permanent impairment` 3 `AWE` EQUALS `none: less than 5% of the whole person is not a permanent impairment (s 70)`
-- Notified of the assessment 1 October 2026: reassessment by 29 October; paid by 12 November if none is sought, or within
-- 28 days of a reassessment notified on 10 December (7 January 2027).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for a reassessment` (`the calendar date` 1 10 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 29 10 2026
#ASSERT `By when must my permanent impairment compensation be paid` (`the calendar date` 1 10 2026) FALSE (`the calendar date` 1 10 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 12 11 2026
#ASSERT `By when must my permanent impairment compensation be paid` (`the calendar date` 1 10 2026) TRUE (`the calendar date` 10 12 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 7 1 2027

§ `What is paid when a worker dies?`

-- Ben's funeral cost $14,500, under the $20,800 cap: $14,500.
#ASSERT `What funeral benefit is paid` 14500 `AWE` EQUALS 14500
-- Spouse and two children: 364 x $2,000 = $728,000; each child 5% = $36,400; the spouse the balance, $655,200.
#ASSERT `What is the spouse's share of the death lump sum` TRUE 2 FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `a share, in dollars, of` 655200
#ASSERT `What is each child's share of the death lump sum` TRUE 2 FALSE `AWE` EQUALS `a share, in dollars, of` 36400
-- Had Ben's mother also depended on him: the Court decides the shares.
#ASSERT `What is the spouse's share of the death lump sum` TRUE 2 TRUE `AWE` EQUALS `as the Court determines on an application under s 62(2)`
-- The 8-year-old's share goes to the Public Trustee until 18.
#ASSERT `Is the child's share paid to the Public Trustee` 8 FALSE
-- Both children are prescribed children: $200 a week each (10% of $2,000).
#ASSERT `Is this child a prescribed child` TRUE 12 FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is this child a prescribed child` TRUE 17 FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `What weekly benefit is paid for each prescribed child` 2 `AWE` EQUALS 200
-- Counselling for the family and a financial adviser, $2,400 in all: paid in full (no cap is prescribed).
#ASSERT `How much of our counselling and financial advice costs is paid` 2400 EQUALS 2400

§ `What must my employer do, and by when, and what if it does not?`

-- Claim received Monday 9 March 2026: forward by Thursday 12 March (3rd working day); decide by Monday 23 March (10th).
#ASSERT `By when must my employer forward my claim` (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) 0 EQUALS `the calendar date` 12 3 2026
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) 0 EQUALS `the calendar date` 23 3 2026
-- Accepted Friday 20 March: payments by Wednesday 25 March (Mon 23, Tue 24, Wed 25).
#ASSERT `By when must my weekly payments start` (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) 0 EQUALS `the calendar date` 25 3 2026
-- Deferred instead, notice posted 20 March: the deferral lasts to 15 May 2026 (56 days).
#ASSERT `Until when can my employer defer my claim` (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 15 5 2026
-- Accepted 20 March: not deemed. Silent on 24 March: deemed accepted under s 87(1).
#ASSERT `Is my employer taken to have accepted my claim` (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) 0 TRUE (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) `accept liability` FALSE (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 24 3 2026) EQUALS `not deemed: the employer notified a decision in time`
#ASSERT `Is my employer taken to have accepted my claim` (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) 0 FALSE (`the calendar date` 24 3 2026) `accept liability` FALSE (`the calendar date` 24 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 24 3 2026) EQUALS `deemed accepted under s 87(1): no decision was notified within 10 working days`
-- Disputed on Friday 20 March without the s 85(8) statement: a decision notified in time, so not deemed, but s 85(8) is
-- not met (finding 18).
#ASSERT `Is my employer taken to have accepted my claim` (`the calendar date` 9 3 2026) 0 TRUE (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` FALSE (`the calendar date` 20 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 24 3 2026) EQUALS `not deemed: the employer notified a dispute in time, but without the s 85(8) statement, so it has not met s 85(8)`
-- Taken to have accepted from 24 March; the employer then notifies acceptance on Friday 27 March: the deemed acceptance
-- runs to the expiry of 14 days after, 10 April. A dispute on 27 March without the statement does not end it.
#ASSERT `Until when is my employer taken to have accepted my claim` `accept liability` (`the calendar date` 27 3 2026) EQUALS `the deemed acceptance runs until the expiry of 14 days after the notice, to` (`the calendar date` 10 4 2026)
#ASSERT `Until when is my employer taken to have accepted my claim` `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` (`the calendar date` 27 3 2026) EQUALS `not ended by this notice: a dispute without the s 85(8) statement is not a notification for s 87(1)(a) and (2)(a) (s 87(3)(b))`
-- The week ending Sunday 15 March: paid by 22 March.
#ASSERT `By when must my employer make this weekly payment` (`the calendar date` 15 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 22 3 2026
-- Earnings information asked for on 10 March: by 31 March. An application to change payments received 1 June: by 15 June.
#ASSERT `By when must my employer give me my earnings information` (`the calendar date` 10 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 31 3 2026
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my application to change my weekly payments` (`the calendar date` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 15 6 2026
-- Dismissed on 1 June 2026 while still off work: an offence. On 3 September, after the 6 months to 2 September: not.
#ASSERT `Would my employer commit an offence by dismissing me` TRUE FALSE (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `the dismissal would be an offence under s 65C (up to 200 penalty units)`
#ASSERT `Would my employer commit an offence by dismissing me` TRUE FALSE (`the calendar date` 2 3 2026) (`the calendar date` 3 9 2026) EQUALS `not an offence under s 65C: outside the 6 months after the injury`
-- The insurer overpaid her by miscalculating: not recoverable unless the Court orders.
#ASSERT `Can an overpayment be recovered from me` TRUE (`the calendar date` 30 4 2026) (`the calendar date` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `not recoverable unless the Court orders otherwise: the employer or insurer calculated the benefit incorrectly (s 74(2)(a))`
-- A late weekly payment by a company employer: up to 125 penalty units.
#ASSERT `What is the most my employer can be fined` `not making a weekly payment in time (s 88(1))` TRUE EQUALS 125
-- A full dispute statement counts; one that omits the interim determination does not.
#ASSERT `Does my employer's dispute notice meet s 85(8)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does my employer's dispute notice meet s 85(8)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)

§ `When must I act?`

-- Her doctor's certificate went in on Thursday 5 March, the claim form by 2 April at the latest; the certificate itself
-- has effect only to 19 March (reg 12(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `By when must I give the second claim document` (`the calendar date` 5 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 2 4 2026
#ASSERT `Until when does my medical certificate have effect` `a medical practitioner` (`the calendar date` 5 3 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 19 3 2026
-- A dispute statement received 1 July 2026: mediation by 29 September. The mediator's certificate received 1 October:
-- Court by 29 October.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for mediation` (`the calendar date` 1 7 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 29 9 2026
#ASSERT `By when must I start Court proceedings` (`the calendar date` 1 10 2026) EQUALS `the calendar date` 29 10 2026
-- An unreasonable refusal of physiotherapy, 20 days' notice and the s 69 statement: the cut may be made. Without the
-- statement: not yet.
#ASSERT `Can my weekly compensation be cut for not undertaking treatment` TRUE 20 TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Can my weekly compensation be cut for not undertaking treatment` TRUE 20 FALSE)
